TLDR;
Don't buy this mess of a broken game, built on nostalgia and hype from the previous game. That has no content what so ever, yet already has several DLC.

Summary;

Do NOT purchase in it's current state at full price, or ever for that matter, get PD2 instead, and evaluate from there based on how much you like it's prequel.

If you insist on purchasing it, do it at 70% sale or more, ever since 2026 begun the playerbase has not broken through the 1000 player barrier on Steam, and at the time of writing this review, it's unlikely this will change since there's no significant changes planned in the roadmap.

For the ones who want a more fleshed out description of why i can't in good faith recommend this game in it's current state;

The Ugly;

-Your savefile is Online only, you don't own your savefile like in PD2, it's all handled Online by the Nebula servers, which means that if the Nebula servers ever get put down, there goes all your progress, and this leads directly into the next point.

-No Offline mode, and SBZ still stringing the community along with false promises for it 2+ years later, it's development is officially shelved, likely as a last resort for the theoretical moment when Online servers/Starbreeze Nebula gets shutdown due to surmounting maintenance costs and a dwindling playerbase, add to this a dwindling number of developers after their recent January-March 2026 layoffs, and we don't even have Community Managers, since the role was tagged for redundancy, so communication is limited to "Social" managers like "PAYDAYgame" which are basically sanitized-corpo speak full of promises, and little substance.

There's also a good amount of preorder exclusive masks and a couple outfits no longer available for purchase, and several sets of Twitch rewards. (These at the very least will likely come back.)

TLDR;

Truly, one of the most Starbreeze games of all time and the distillation of Bo Andersson's decade long power trip, don't waste your money until (if at all) there's an Offline mode, the few developers that remain are trying their best, but they are generally too scared to make significant changes due to previous backlash so new changes come across as too little-too late, and all the heavy hitters that made PD2 into a staple, like Ulf Andersson, Simon Viklund, and the community managers like Almir and Elisabeth among many others have either left, been pushed out, or rearranged to other less impactful positions.

